#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453} :activity/id #uuid "4cb8dd70-6479-5b35-b723-3890d781528c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048388 :topic/id #uuid "9b5fc10c-ada7-55cd-8d40-6c4729395195" :topic/math-academy-id 3693} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179692 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179551 :question/id #uuid "055422e3-fb8a-5579-ab97-b946d5687e30" :question/math-academy-id "e-9052"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "093a897b-2422-5e5b-b713-c8cd61a702dc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050903}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489262}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points Where a Particle Is at Instantaneous Rest"} :step/id #uuid "ec16918e-d414-592f-ad99-94fed4da6b99" :step/math-academy-id 33784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179552 :question/id #uuid "a4d988d6-4181-588c-8e11-02a96554a76a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9053"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "709d94b3-ff84-58ed-80b1-b2c687eaf648" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180098} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365246} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496238}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Value"} :step/id #uuid "c3cafc43-ac5d-5524-9bd0-e3b82ec62fd4" :step/math-academy-id 33785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180703 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate a particle's velocity and displacement when its acceleration is a function of *displacement.* In this lesson, we'll use this knowledge to compute various properties of a particle in motion.  \n\nSuppose a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. Its displacement from the origin is $x\\,\\rm{m},$ its velocity is $v\\,\\rm{m/s},$ and its acceleration is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2.$ \n\n![](images/52/5222b12a7f08a33dc981ac95bd717cce945e1d2e3565fb3631f108fd0f307a12.png)\n\nFurthermore, suppose $P$ has velocity $2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when its displacement is $\\sqrt{3}\\,\\rm{m}.$ \n\nLet's use this information to determine at which values of $x$ the particle is instantaneously at rest.\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2x\\,\\rm{m/s}^2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} =2x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t}= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}v^2\\right) = 2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x} \\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) \\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int 2x \\:\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2+ C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\,\\rm{m/s}$ when $x=\\sqrt 3\\,\\rm{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12v^2 = x^2-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the particle is instantaneously at rest when $v(x) = 0\\,\\rm{m/s}.$ We find the displacement of the particle when $P$ is instantaneously at rest by substituting $v=0$ in the equation above and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dcdbb7a7-457c-5386-9a73-ae4d9da40f89" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5769 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "28c57294-3b4c-56e0-8cac-331a28850e07" :step/math-academy-id 35399 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179715 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179577 :question/id #uuid "58325462-3a69-575e-bdfb-31db815b0d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9456"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8c812adc-7418-5a76-9c7f-96c726c7ca0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186489224} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496244} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496284}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Maximum Speed of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "1a2b4d25-22b2-5303-9d34-924730dd694c" :step/math-academy-id 35400}] :activity/title "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179847}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496351} :activity/id #uuid "a2048220-14aa-5206-bbbd-f88860b43a2a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048388 :topic/id #uuid "9b5fc10c-ada7-55cd-8d40-6c4729395195" :topic/math-academy-id 3693} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ebcf16d-17e2-521f-84af-8c80fc770e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365331} {:answer/id #uuid "f51a32aa-8b15-5397-a3a9-3d850bb58c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365332} {:answer/id #uuid "3bea1d31-74b4-5ddf-8bc9-1bd6d0bd85b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365333} {:answer/id #uuid "b4036230-717e-5222-ab98-3a9fdf09553e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365334} {:answer/id #uuid "8832e1f0-db88-5028-8c7f-f4ff514e3d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365335}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365334} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1e0c06-6d19-533b-b17a-f929a1be5595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "19e94881-dc11-5b6a-8172-59477ad89f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-163565" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the positive $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of the acceleration is $(10+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant, and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=12\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle has a velocity of $2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $4\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a9/a967d26d7c139b5316c57b5546f47e52b30b2ff9e2d25620c79d05843cf5bfce.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $(10+kx)\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-(10+kx).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-(10+kx).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int -(10+kx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=12\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(12)^{2} & =-10(0)-\\frac{k(0)^{2}}{2}+C \\\\ C & =72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=72-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=2\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s$ when $x=4\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =72-10x-\\frac{k{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{15})^{2} & =72-10(4)-\\frac{k(4)^{2}}{2} \\\\ 30 & =72-40-8k \\\\ 8k & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2f006507-cb5e-5231-8769-1e44e53b48f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496351 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496238 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c3d3836-b51f-5e7c-a7a9-704639b3e9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495608} {:answer/id #uuid "528dca58-912a-5c4e-81e7-257ba6b0db7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495698} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4182f5-2803-552b-853e-c778c967ac69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495746} {:answer/id #uuid "c37c19ae-e778-56f2-937e-0465119bfe29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495938} {:answer/id #uuid "c8690093-4909-568c-bd3a-20b77037a0fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495948}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495938} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78871b-3da5-52c1-a57c-cb87914fafa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2f22631e-a7c9-5925-8b08-e1ce77a0788a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163499" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ at the origin and the particle is at rest when its displacement is $6\\,m,$ find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9a/9ac139d8823f326f32869f308cb2d04cdc9e0d86dba7d4a11b66955afd665da3.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k{x}^{2}(x-5)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k{x}^{2}(x-5).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =k(\\frac{(0)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(0)^{3}}{3})+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $v=0\\,m/s$ when $x=6\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5{x}^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2} & =k(\\frac{(6)^{4}}{4}-\\frac{5(6)^{3}}{3})+6 \\\\ 0 & =k(324-360)+6 \\\\ 0 & =-36k+6 \\\\ 36k & =6 \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6298506a-6ab4-5316-a7ce-7e5bfd41dc85" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496382}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02c1d6a8-802b-5aac-9cd8-94b55465e8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{15}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495515} {:answer/id #uuid "382ad457-c78c-5db2-b511-049b1e51f672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495644} {:answer/id #uuid "95d62877-62ed-576b-b8c1-68b72d5fa0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495844} {:answer/id #uuid "dfda9d6c-2aba-514b-b5cf-e34408029568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496007} {:answer/id #uuid "f81daac3-e6be-5bee-9268-19f1a228f0ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{22}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496051}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496007} :answer-field/id #uuid "10849e2a-d2b8-5eda-a82b-458c2096cbcd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8f6a9cc3-415e-5254-bb6f-82cbdb814536" :question/math-academy-id "q-163566" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=8\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $1\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/73da2be2514373e6d0b1052f0eca21c339b4d826d38621faf72b3e251d7d8a95.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(4-2x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(4-2x) & =0 \\\\ 4-2x & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2k>0$ | $0$ | $-2k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=2\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(4-2x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(4-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(4(0)-(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(4x-{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=8\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8)^{2} & =k(4(1)-{(1)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 32 & =3k+8 \\\\ 3k & =24 \\\\ k & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8(4x-{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =32x-8{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-16{x}^{2}+64x+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+4x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(2)=4\\sqrt{-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1}=4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9d41f50b-d402-55a6-b472-184dbf89c69d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496382 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f9bb39-d662-5b7f-b26c-4c6a27fffc10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} {:answer/id #uuid "40369435-8306-5137-8429-a26ad39afe91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365291} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ede10b-ba4c-5e79-b731-b8038347c020" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365292} {:answer/id #uuid "5a09cbc5-7125-5f56-932d-0251a7308a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365293} {:answer/id #uuid "07428674-2986-5965-9daa-705a8555f7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365294}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365290} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e05aabc-648f-50bd-bc69-595d4091baa3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365289}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ecee2124-1bd8-5650-8e8b-4c51642487c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163553" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/61/61388466bb2d97b5080707e769ae862e633bfb0fe1d2b5f16c75daa14da76e53.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(6-6x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=6-6x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-6x & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $2$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $3>0$ | $0$ | $-6<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=1\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=6-6x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (6-6x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=6x-3{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & =6(0)-3(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =6x-3{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =12x-6{x}^{2}+8 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-6{x}^{2}+12x+8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(1)=\\sqrt{-6(1)^{2}+12(1)+8}=\\sqrt{14}\\,m/s\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c9baaed7-d44a-530e-b75a-7fd6fd6eb3d1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496244 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f39b3f3-f271-59a4-bf86-3f809a0727f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495577} {:answer/id #uuid "45fc70ca-1697-5db6-b1f1-c08ca6e6db5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495667} {:answer/id #uuid "6b84233d-39b9-5907-bb7e-b896ae67e3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{53}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495748} {:answer/id #uuid "b2128b20-1288-5387-bc4f-30b80ffe9c21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495911} {:answer/id #uuid "f55ad6c7-9522-5c97-be01-f1f507abc917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496043}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495667} :answer-field/id #uuid "096d2af6-6379-5a37-9ed1-9857796a70e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3aa3a140-5e95-5af8-9fd5-684dff535064" :question/math-academy-id "q-163561" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ where $k$ is a positive constant. If $P$ has velocity $v=4\\,m/s$ at the origin and has velocity $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when its displacement is $2\\,m,$ find the maximum velocity of the particle." :question/worked-solution "![](images/60/602ef5480fe59ccf921d17a19d10848a1a8a04a1fc3fb3181b8e9b17ebe81fad.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $k(7-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(7-x) & =0 \\\\ 7-x & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,7)$ | $7$ | $(7,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $6$ |  | $8$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $k>0$ | $0$ | $-k<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=7\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=k(7-x).\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int k(7-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =k(7(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})+C \\\\ C & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8.\n$$\n\nAlso, since $v=2\\sqrt{10}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,m,$ we can find the value of the positive constant $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =k(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(2\\sqrt{10})^{2} & =k(7(2)-\\frac{1}{2}{(2)}^{2})+8 \\\\ 20 & =12k+8 \\\\ 12k & =12 \\\\ k & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =1(7x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})+8 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =14x-{x}^{2}+16 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+14x+16}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(7)=\\sqrt{-(7)^{2}+14(7)+16}=\\sqrt{65}\\,m/s\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fc3bb8d9-68c2-5b90-9003-ab8666f160bc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496341}}] :activity/title "Determining Properties of Objects Described as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495504}]}
